"One Way ANOVA: Gaps, Spreads and False Alarms | VirtualDemos",
"One Way ANOVA: Gaps, Spreads and False Alarms",
"Student laboratory · Statistics for physics",
"Textbook: OpenStax <em>Introductory Statistics 2e</em>, §§{#} to {#} (one way ANOVA, the F distribution and the F ratio, facts about the F distribution, the test of two variances, and the chapter’s ANOVA lab). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/introductory-statistics-2e.",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Three ways to time a pendulum; Four lab groups measure g; Five batches of {#} Ω resistors; Three fertilizers and plant growth; All means equal: false alarms; Two variances: is the new thermometer steadier?; Two variances: two stopwatch timers.",
"Each column of dots is one group’s sample. The thick bar is the group’s mean, and the bracket with two open circles marks one standard deviation s above and below it. The dashed line across is the grand mean of all the points. Thin gray lines join each point to its own group’s mean (the within groups scatter); the thick colored lines join each group mean to the grand mean (the between groups differences).",
"Drag a mean bar up or down, or drag an open circle to stretch or squeeze a group. Or use the sliders in the <strong>Means and spreads</strong> card: <strong>Gap between means</strong> spaces the group means evenly that far apart, and <strong>Spread s within groups</strong> gives every group that standard deviation. Click a slider’s number to type a value. <strong>Points per group n</strong> and <strong>Groups k</strong> are in <strong>The groups</strong> card; <strong>New draw</strong> gives the groups new shapes.",
"Beside the dots, two squares have the two mean squares as their areas: between groups and within groups. Their ratio is F. The ANOVA table under the picture lists SS, df, MS, F and the <strong>p value</strong>, with the decision at the <strong>Significance level α</strong> in <strong>The test</strong> card.",
"<strong>Play</strong> runs {#} studies in which every group comes from populations with the same mean (H₀ true), and fills the histogram of their F values under the F curve. The line under it tallies <strong>Null studies</strong>, <strong>False alarms</strong> (F beyond the critical value) and the <strong>Fraction</strong>. Changing α recounts the same studies.",
"<strong>Two variances</strong> (top of the picture, or the two variances setups) compares the spreads of two groups with F = s₁²/s₂².",
"<strong>Record reading</strong> stores the settings, F, the p value and the tally of the null studies. <strong>Export to worksheet</strong> fills the Data tables below; <strong>Clear</strong> starts a new series. All data are simulated; Draw {#} is the same for everyone.",
"See what the F ratio of a one way ANOVA measures: make the gaps between group means bigger and the scatter within groups smaller and watch F grow as a ratio of two variances. Then run many studies with every mean equal and count how often ANOVA raises a false alarm.",
"<strong>The question.</strong> You have k groups (three ways of timing a pendulum, five batches of resistors) with \\(n_j\\) measurements each. Are the population means all equal? H₀: \\(\\mu_1 = \\mu_2 = \\cdots = \\mu_k\\). Hₐ: not all the means are equal. Comparing the groups two at a time with t tests would raise the chance of a false alarm with every comparison; ANOVA asks the question once.",
"<strong>Two estimates of one variance.</strong> If H₀ is true and every group has the same population variance σ², there are two independent ways to estimate σ²:",
"<strong>Within groups.</strong> Each group’s own variance \\(s_j^2\\) estimates σ², however far apart the means are. Pooled: \\(MS_{within} = \\frac{SS_{within} }{N - k}, \\quad SS_{within} = \\sum_j (n_j - 1)s_j^2,\\) with N the total number of points.",
"<strong>Between groups.</strong> The group means scatter about the grand mean \\(\\bar{x}\\); if H₀ is true that scatter comes only from chance, and \\(MS_{between} = \\frac{SS_{between} }{k - 1}, \\quad SS_{between} = \\sum_j n_j(\\bar{x}_j - \\bar{x})^2,\\) also estimates σ². If the means really differ, \\(MS_{between}\\) is larger.",
"<strong>The F ratio.</strong> \\(F = MS_{between}/MS_{within}\\). When H₀ is true, F is near {#} and follows the F distribution with \\(df_{num} = k - 1\\) and \\(df_{denom} = N - k\\). The F distribution is never negative and is skewed to the right; its mean is \\(df_{denom}/(df_{denom} - 2)\\), a little more than {#}. The p value is the area to the right of your F, so ANOVA is always a right tailed test. ANOVA assumes each population is normal, the samples are independent, and the population variances are equal.",
"<strong>Equal gaps.</strong> For k = {#} groups of n points each, with means a gap d apart and every group’s standard deviation s, \\(SS_{between} = n\\,(d^2 + 0 + d^2) = 2nd^2\\), so \\(MS_{between} = nd^2\\) and \\(MS_{within} = s^2\\):",
"\\[F = \\frac{n d^2}{s^2}.\\]",
"<strong>The test of two variances.</strong> To ask whether two populations have the same variance, \\(F = s_1^2 / s_2^2\\) with df \\(n_1 - 1\\) and \\(n_2 - 1\\). Values far above {#} or far below {#} are evidence the variances differ.",
"Three groups of n = {#} have means {#}, {#} and {#} ms and each has s = {#} ms. Compute \\(MS_{between}\\), \\(MS_{within}\\) and F from the formulas above.",
"What are \\(df_{num}\\) and \\(df_{denom}\\) for k = {#} groups of {#}? For {#} groups of {#}?",
"Predict before you look: if you double the gap between the means and keep the spread, by what factor will F change? If you double the spread and keep the gap? And when every mean is equal and you run {#} studies at α = {#}, about how many will give a false alarm? Write your guesses and one sentence of reasoning for each.",
"<strong>A. The gap between means.</strong> Press the setup <strong>Three ways to time a pendulum</strong> (k = {#}, n = {#}, spread s = {#} ms). In the <strong>Means and spreads</strong> card, set <strong>Gap between means</strong> to {#} (click its number and type {#}) and press <strong>Record reading</strong> in the Readings section. Repeat with a gap of {#}, {#}, {#}, {#} and {#} ms, recording each time. Watch the between groups square grow. Press <strong>Export to worksheet</strong> (Table {#}), then <strong>Clear</strong>. In Table {#}, fill the column Gap² for each row.",
"<strong>B. The spread within groups.</strong> Set <strong>Gap between means</strong> to {#} ms. Set <strong>Spread s within groups</strong> to {#} ms and record; repeat with {#}, {#}, {#} and {#} ms. Watch the within groups square grow. Export to worksheet (Table {#}), then Clear. Fill the column {#}/s² for each row.",
"<strong>C. False alarms.</strong> Press the setup <strong>All means equal: false alarms</strong> (every group mean equal, so H₀ is true). Press <strong>Play</strong> and wait for the {#} null studies to finish. With <strong>Significance level α</strong> at {#} record a reading; then choose {#} and record; then {#} and record. Then set α to {#}, choose <strong>Groups k</strong> = {#}, press <strong>Play</strong>, and record when it ends. Finally set <strong>Groups k</strong> = {#} and <strong>Points per group n</strong> = {#}, press <strong>Play</strong>, and record when it ends. Export to worksheet (Table {#}), then Clear.",
"<strong>D. Two variances.</strong> Press the setup <strong>Two variances: is the new thermometer steadier?</strong> (old thermometer s₁ = {#} °C, new s₂ = {#} °C, ten readings each, right tailed). Record a reading. Then set <strong>Spread s₁ of group {#}</strong> to {#} °C and record again. Export to worksheet (Table {#}).",
